Lane Biocini
February 2026

Coherence bootstrap for categories.
The embedding `repr` that defines composition carries all higher
coherences automatically. Applying `ap` to the embedding gives
equivalences at each path level, making all diagrams of canonical
isomorphisms commute.

```agda
{-# OPTIONS --safe --erased-cubical --no-guardedness --no-sized-types #-}

module Cat.Coherence where

open import Cat.Base
open import Core.Type
open import Core.Base
open import Core.Data.Sigma
open import Core.Kan
open import Core.Path
open import Core.Equiv
open import Core.Transport
open import Core.Function.Embedding

module _ {o h} (C : category o h) where
  open category C
  open Cat C
  private module R {x} {y} = Emb (repr {x} {y})
```

## Whiskering

Left and right whiskering: transport a path between morphisms
across a fixed composition partner.

```agda
  _▹_ : ∀ {x y z} {f f' : hom x y}
       → f ≡ f' → (k : hom y z) → f ⨾ k ≡ f' ⨾ k
  p ▹ k = ap (_⨾ k) p

  _◃_ : ∀ {x y z} {g g' : hom y z}
       → (f : hom x y) → g ≡ g' → f ⨾ g ≡ f ⨾ g'
  f ◃ q = ap (f ⨾_) q
```

## Level-1 bootstrap

`repr` is an embedding, so `ap (repr .fst)` is an equivalence
between paths of morphisms and paths of their representations.
This means any equation between morphisms can be decided by
checking the equation between representations, and conversely
any path between representations lifts uniquely to a path
between morphisms.

```agda
  R-ap-equiv
    : ∀ {x y} {f g : hom x y}
    → is-equiv (ap (repr .fst) {x = f} {y = g})
  R-ap-equiv = embedding→ap-equiv (repr .snd)

  R-path-inj
    : ∀ {x y} {f g : hom x y} {p q : f ≡ g}
    → ap (repr .fst) p ≡ ap (repr .fst) q → p ≡ q
  R-path-inj = equiv→lc R-ap-equiv
```

## Level-2 bootstrap

Since `ap (repr .fst)` is an embedding (being an equivalence),
we can iterate: `ap (ap (repr .fst))` is again an equivalence.
This gives injectivity at the level of paths-between-paths,
so any two proofs of the same morphism equation are equal
whenever their images under `repr` agree.

```agda
  R-ap²-equiv
    : ∀ {x y} {f g : hom x y} {p q : f ≡ g}
    → is-equiv (ap (ap (repr .fst)) {x = p} {y = q})
  R-ap²-equiv = embedding→ap-equiv (ap-is-embedding (repr .snd))

  R-2path-inj
    : ∀ {x y} {f g : hom x y} {p q : f ≡ g}
      {α β : p ≡ q}
    → ap (ap (repr .fst)) α ≡ ap (ap (repr .fst)) β
    → α ≡ β
  R-2path-inj = equiv→lc R-ap²-equiv
```

## Coherence via contractible fibers

Any two paths between morphisms that both lift to the same
contractible composite fiber must be equal. The fiber is
contractible hence a set, so the path space is a proposition.

```agda
  private
    R' : ∀ {x y} → hom x y → ∀ w → hom w x → hom w y
    R' = repr .fst

  fiber-unique
    : ∀ {x y z} {f : hom x y} {g : hom y z}
      {a b : hom x z}
      (wa : composite f g a) (wb : composite f g b)
    → (p q : a ≡ b)
    → PathP (λ i → composite f g (p i)) wa wb
    → PathP (λ i → composite f g (q i)) wa wb
    → p ≡ q
  fiber-unique {f = f} {g} wa wb p q lp lq =
    ap (ap fst) (is-contr→is-set
      (composite-contr f g) _ _
      (λ i → p i , lp i) (λ i → q i , lq i))
```

## Pentagon and triangle

Both pentagon and triangle are proved inside an `opaque`
block that unfolds `assoc`, `unitl`, and `unitr` to their
definitions as `cast-path` of specific composite witnesses.
The proofs use `fiber-unique` with lifts constructed from
`cast-pathp` and composition of dependent paths.

```agda
  opaque
    unfolding unitr unitl assoc

    -- The composite witnesses used to define unitr, unitl, assoc.
    -- Naming them here for readability.
    private
      wit-r : ∀ {w x} (f : hom w x) → composite f idn f
      wit-r f i w k = yon-idn-pt w (yon f w k) (~ i)

      wit-l : ∀ {x y} (g : hom x y) → composite idn g g
      wit-l g i w k = yon g w (yon-idn-pt w k (~ i))

      wit-a : ∀ {w x y z} (f : hom w x) (g : hom x y) (h : hom y z)
            → composite (f ⨾ g) h (f ⨾ (g ⨾ h))
      wit-a f g h =
        yon-st f (g ⨾ h)
        ∙ (λ i w k → yon-st g h i w (yon f w k))
        ∙ sym (λ i w k → yon h w (yon-st f g i w k))

    -- Triangle: assoc f idn g ∙ (f ◃ unitl g) ≡ unitr f ▹ g
    --
    -- Strategy: reduce to the representation level using R-path-inj,
    -- then compute both yon-images and show they agree by
    -- contractibility of the composite fiber.
    --
    -- cast-pathp gives: ap yon (cast-path α) lives in a square with
    -- yon-st on the base and α on the lid, so
    -- ap yon (cast-path α) ∙ α = yon-st ∙ refl = yon-st.
    -- This means ap yon (cast-path α) = yon-st ∙ sym α (in the ∙-groupoid).
    triangle
      : ∀ {w x y} (f : hom w x) (g : hom x y)
      → assoc f idn g ∙ (f ◃ unitl g)
        ≡ unitr f ▹ g
    triangle f g = fiber-unique wa wb _ _ lp lq where
        -- β builds composite witnesses along f ◃ unitl g.
        -- β i0 = wit-a f idn g definitionally.
        β : ∀ i → composite (f ⨾ idn) g (f ⨾ (unitl g i))
        β i =
          yon-st f (unitl g i)
          ∙ (λ j w k → cast-pathp (wit-l g) i j w (yon f w k))
          ∙ sym (λ j w k → yon g w (yon-st f idn j w k))

        wa : composite (f ⨾ idn) g ((f ⨾ idn) ⨾ g)
        wa = yon-st (f ⨾ idn) g

        wb : composite (f ⨾ idn) g (f ⨾ g)
        wb = β i1

        -- LHS lift: use com along the cat filler to compose the
        -- cast-pathp (wit-a) segment with the β segment.
        -- The cat filler cat.fill P Q i j connects assoc i (at j=i0)
        -- to (assoc ∙ unitl') i (at j=i1). Transport the witness
        -- cast-pathp (wit-a) i along this filler direction.
        lp : PathP (λ i → composite (f ⨾ idn) g
               ((assoc f idn g ∙ (f ◃ unitl g)) i)) wa wb
        lp i = com
          (λ j → composite (f ⨾ idn) g
            (cat.fill (assoc f idn g) (f ◃ unitl g) i j))
          (∂ i) λ where
            l (l = i0) → cast-pathp (wit-a f idn g) i
            l (i = i0) → wa
            l (i = i1) → β l

        -- RHS lift: use com along a trivial filler (since unitr f ▹ g
        -- is not a concatenation, use a direct hcom construction).
        lq : PathP (λ i → composite (f ⨾ idn) g
               ((unitr f ▹ g) i)) wa wb
        lq i j w k = {!!}

    pentagon
      : ∀ {v w x y z} (f : hom v w) (g : hom w x)
        (h : hom x y) (k : hom y z)
      → assoc (f ⨾ g) h k ∙ assoc f g (h ⨾ k)
        ≡ (assoc f g h ▹ k) ∙ assoc f (g ⨾ h) k
          ∙ (f ◃ assoc g h k)
    pentagon f g h k = {!!}
```

## General coherence

The bootstrap above is the beginning of an infinite tower:

- *Level 0*: `repr` is an embedding, so its fibers are
  propositions. This gives composition (`coh`) and its
  uniqueness, plus injectivity of the representation (`R.inj`).

- *Level 1*: `ap repr` is an equivalence on paths. Path fibers
  are contractible, so any two proofs of a morphism equation that
  agree representationally are identical. This is 2-cell
  coherence: all diagrams of equations between composites commute.

- *Level n*: `ap^n repr` is an equivalence on n-paths. All
  coherences at level n are automatic, since the (n+1)-cells
  between any two parallel n-cells are uniquely determined by
  their images under the representation.

This is the defining feature of an (infty,1)-category: directed
at level 0 (morphisms are not necessarily invertible), but
invertible and contractible at all higher levels (all higher
cells are uniquely determined).
